  "query_preview": "..\n\nUpper level ridging will continue to be the dominant weather \nfeature of our weather over the upcoming week, keeping us \ngenerally warm and dry save for a few opportunities for afternoon \nthunderstorms. Heat will continue to be the most widespread hazard\nthrough the weekend as the ridge remains positioned in a way that\nkeeps us in southwesterly flow aloft. This heat will peak on \nSaturday, even as a shortwave moving over Washington will kick off\na few afternoon showers and thunderstorms. More details on the \nheat and thunderstorms can be found below in the Confidence and \nScenarios sections. \n\nTemperatures will lessen slightly Sunday as the ridge wanes \nslightly, but temperatures will remain hot with most lower \nelevations reaching the mid 90s to near 100. Temperatures will \nwane slightly further Monday as the ridge continues to wane a bit,\nbut by Monday the ridge will settle into a state it will more or \nless maintain its strength through the remainder of the week. The \nmain day to day difference will be a few rounds of monsoonal \nmoisture working through the area, which may allow for afternoon \nthunderstorms to pop up. As of now, the best chances for afternoon\nthunderstorms outside of today and Saturday will be after \nWednesday, but I would not rule out a storm popping up any day \nover the next week.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nHeat through Sunday: The ongoing heat wave will continue through \nSunday as the upper level ridge maintains its strength. The main \nquestion will be just how hot does it get during the day, and to \nwhat degree does it cool off overnight. Raw model output seems to \nbe fairly consistent at keeping lower elevation locations across \nthe plains near 100 today, 100-107 Saturday, then upper 90s to \nnear 100 on Sunday. However, there are a few areas of uncertainty.\nThe first wild card is the lingering smoke over the area. While \nit seems to have kept temperatures a bit warmer overnight, it \nhas not seemed to slow down our warm up today as many lower \nelevation locations have shot up to the mid/upper 90s to near 100 \nas of 3PM with plenty of daytime left for warming. We will see how\nit affects our cooldown tonight and temperatures Saturday, but \nright now it seems to have more potential to keep us warmer at \nnight while not affecting the temperature during the day too much.\nThe other, more significant wild card for Saturday will be some \nafternoon convection, which would quickly cool off any areas that \nsee thunderstorms and/or their outflows, which may keep them from \nreaching their true temperature potential provided the storms move\nthrough before mid to late afternoon. \n\nThunderstorms today and Saturday: Showers and thunderstorms will\nremain possible the next two days, today mostly across far\nSouthwestern Montana, with more widespread thunderstorms possible\non Saturday. Given the very warm and dry environment, any\nthunderstorm is liable to produce little rainfall on the ground,\nand may also produce strong outflows. This is especially true on\nSaturday, when storms will have just enough shear to get\norganized, and dry air aloft may help promote processes that\nproduce downburst winds and their associated outflows. Ludwig",
